ue}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-28","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-01","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-05","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-16","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-06-24","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-06-29","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-13","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Boston audit finds governance gaps amid budget pressure","lead":"Boston's internal audit has found serious gaps in how the council manages financial risk. Five actions from a prior audit remain overdue, some by more than a year, while a critical audit of the council's savings programme has not been started halfway through the financial year. A review of the finance team's capacity and capability has been waiting for management to respond to its draft report since March. These governance failures matter because the council faces sustained high-risk pressure to balance its long-term budget with government funding falling and service costs rising, and no improvement is planned. The audit committee heard more positive news: treasury investments outperformed budget by \u00a3169,389 in Q1, and delays in spending on buildings and equipment have left the council with more cash available for investment.","iso":"2026-09-28","paper_title":"Q1 Treasury Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk/documents/s26668/26668.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-28","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","paper_title":"Q1 Treasury Report 2026-27 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk/documents/s26669/26669.pdf","headline":"Property fund portfolio carrying \u00a32.85m (16.81%) cumulative loss on \u00a316.99m invested since 2016-18","detail":"The council holds property fund units purchased 2016-2018 with original cost of \u00a316,989,766 and current net asset value of \u00a314,138,540. Cumulative loss is \u00a32,851,226. Largest losses are Schroder (-27.16%), AEW (-21.67%), BlackRock (-10.35%), and Threadneedle (-12.24%). Only M&G is liquidating and has returned a gain of \u00a3124,317. Fair value movements are reversed through reserves each year-end. Q1 quarterly movement showed a further \u00a3172,951 decrease in NAV.","category":"capital","matters":"The council has deployed substantial capital into property-backed investments at a poor point in the market cycle; whilst fair-value losses are reversed through reserves, the underlying capital allocation reflects poor judgment and ongoing exposure to further potential decline","figures":"Total invested: \u00a316,989,766; current NAV: \u00a314,138,540; cumulative loss: \u00a32,851,226 (16.81%); Q1 decrease: \u00a3172,951; M&G distributions received: \u00a34,124,317 on \u00a34m (gain \u00a3124,317)","quote":"The Council purchased property fund units between 2016 and 2018. The overall change in the combined Net Asset Values for all funds during the first quarter of 2026/27 has been a decrease of \u00a3172,951","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-28","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","paper_title":"Q1 Treasury Report 2026-27 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk/documents/s26669/26669.pdf","headline":"Council plans to borrow \u00a33m from October 2026, nearly quadrupling its debt from \u00a31m","detail":"Boston currently has \u00a31m of PWLB debt (4.81%, maturing July 2030). The council has budgeted to take \u00a33m of short-term borrowing from 1 October 2026 at an estimated rate of 3.50%. This would increase gross external debt from \u00a31m to \u00a34m. The quoted rate of 3.50% is notably below current PWLB certainty rates (5.98% on 30 June 2026), suggesting use of money market borrowing.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A tripling of debt from \u00a31m to \u00a34m is a material change to the council's financial risk profile and leverage position, even if short-term; clarity is needed on the source, duration, and purpose of this borrowing","figures":"Current debt: \u00a31m PWLB at 4.81% (maturity 15 July 2030); planned new borrowing: \u00a33m from 1 October 2026 at estimated 3.50%; forecast gross external debt: \u00a34,000k","quote":"The Council has budgeted to take \u00a33m of new short term borrowing from 1 October 2026 at an estimated rate of 3.50%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-28","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","paper_title":"Q1 Treasury Report 2026-27 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk/documents/s26669/26669.pdf","headline":"Investment income forecast to exceed budget by \u00a3647,511 (80%), but entirely dependent on temporary factors","detail":"The council's investment income forecast outturn is \u00a31,453,491 against a budget of \u00a3805,980. The council has explicitly attributed this \u00a3647,511 favorable variance to two temporary conditions: (1) market interest rates being higher than the 2026/27 budget assumed, and (2) elevated cash balances resulting from capital programme slippage. Treasury investments achieved 4.45% return and property funds 3.80%.","category":"income","matters":"This substantial outperformance (80% above budget) will reverse once capital spending resumes and rates fall; a lender must discount this from the council's sustainable financial position","figures":"Budget \u00a3805,980 vs forecast outturn \u00a31,453,491; variance \u00a3647,511 favorable; rates achieved: treasury 4.45%, property funds 3.80%, combined 4.15%","quote":"The higher level of investment income achieved compared to the original budget is due to interest rates in the market being higher than the budgeted return for 2026/27 and balances available for investment being higher due to slippage in the 2025/26 capital programme","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-28","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","paper_title":"Q1 Treasury Report 2026-27 - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk/documents/s26669/26669.pdf","headline":"Capital programme severely underspent in Q1, with only 14% of annual budget delivered","detail":"Capital expenditure reached just \u00a34,211k of a \u00a330,596k budget in the quarter. The council explicitly identifies this underspend as creating backlogs of unspent funding. This follows noted slippage in the prior year (2025/26), suggesting the under-delivery may be structural rather than one-off.","category":"capital","matters":"A capital programme running at 14% of forecast pace through Q1 raises questions about the council's ability to deliver its strategic and service plans on schedule, and may indicate planning or resource constraints that require intervention","figures":"Capital expenditure: \u00a34,211k Q1 actual vs \u00a330,596k budget (13.8% delivery rate)","quote":"balances available for investment being higher due to slippage in the 2025/26 capital programme","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-28","committee":"Audit & Governance Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 2 - skills audit survey - collective results","paper_url":"https://democracy.boston.gov.uk/documents/s26677/26677.pdf","headline":"Half of audit committee members have only basic knowledge or no experience in Treasury Management","detail":"The audit identifies Treasury Management as a main development area, with ratings showing 17% of members report no experience and 33% report basic knowledge\u2014totalling 50% with less than intermediate knowledge. Counter Fraud shows a similar pattern: 8% no experience, 42% basic knowledge.","category":"governance","matters":"A lender assessing council finances would want the audit committee overseeing borrowing, investments, and cash management to have strong Treasury Management knowledge. A knowledge gap here represents a constraint on oversight of decisions material to lending risk.","figures":"Treasury Management \u2013 1 \u2013 17%, 2 \u2013 33%, 3 \u2013 25%, 4 \u2013 25%. Counter Fraud \u2013 1 \u2013 8%, 2 \u2013 42%, 3 \u2013 25%, 4 \u2013 25%.","quote":"Treasury Management and Counter Fraud are the main areas for development, with around half of respondents reporting only basic knowledge or no experience.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-13"},"name":"Boston BC","findings_total":82,"news_drafts_total":1}}